        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Clean and brighten first. On a sound deck that is the whole prep, and it gives penetrating stain the open grain it needs. Reach for a sander only when a film finish will not strip, when pressure washing has furred the boards, when weathered wood needs to look new, when new boards fail the water test, or for rails and spot repairs.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            When you do sand, wash first, dry for 48 hours, stay between 36 and 80 grit, treat whole boards, remove every trace of dust, and never put a sander anywhere near composite. Done that way, sanding is a useful tool. Done any other way, it is the reason your stain will not dry.
          </p>
        </div>

